<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-underdamped-direction-changes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing Underdamped Motion</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PREREQ REMARK: ARCTAN REVIEW
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <remark xml:id="remark-arctan-review">
    <title>Prerequisite: Solving <m>\tan(\theta) = A</m></title>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Solving <m>\tan(\theta) = A</m>.</alert>
            Taking arctan of both sides gives one solution,
            <m>\theta = \arctan(A)</m>, but this is not the only one.
            Since <m>\tan</m> has period <m>\pi</m>, shifting any solution by
            <m>\pi</m> gives another solution. The complete solution set is:
            <me>\theta = \arctan(A) + n\pi, \quad n = 0, \pm 1, \pm 2, \ldots</me>
          </p>
          <justification>
            <title>Why does this give all solutions?</title>
            <p>
              Because <m>\tan(\theta)</m> has period <m>\pi</m>, the equation
              <m>\tan(\theta) = A</m> is satisfied by exactly one value of
              <m>\theta</m> in each interval of length <m>\pi</m>. The function
              <m>\arctan</m> is the inverse of <m>\tan</m> restricted to
              <m>(-\pi/2,\, \pi/2)</m>, so <m>\arctan(A)</m> picks out the
              unique solution in that interval. Every other solution is obtained
              by adding an integer multiple of <m>\pi</m>, covering all intervals.
            </p>
          </justification>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Solving <m>\tan(at) = A</m> — which values of <m>n</m> give <m>t \geq 0</m>?</alert>
            Setting <m>\theta = at</m> and applying the result above:
            <me>t = \frac{\arctan(A) + n\pi}{a}, \quad n = 0, \pm 1, \pm 2, \ldots</me>
            Since we need <m>t \geq 0</m>, we keep only values of <m>n</m> that make
            the right-hand side nonnegative. The key fact is that
            <m>\arctan(A)</m> always has the same sign as <m>A</m>,
            with output in <m>(-\pi/2,\, \pi/2)</m>. This gives two cases:
            <ul>
              <li><p>
                <alert>Case 1: <m>A \geq 0</m>.</alert>
                Then <m>\arctan(A) \geq 0</m>, so <m>\arctan(A) + n\pi \geq 0</m>
                for all <m>n \geq 0</m>. Valid solutions: <m>n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</m>
              </p></li>
              <li><p>
                <alert>Case 2: <m>A \lt 0</m>.</alert>
                Then <m>\arctan(A) \in (-\pi/2, 0)</m>, so <m>n = 0</m> gives
                a negative value of <m>t</m> (not valid). But since
                <m>|\arctan(A)| \lt \pi/2 \lt \pi</m>, adding one full period
                gives <m>\arctan(A) + \pi > 0</m>. Valid solutions: <m>n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots</m>
              </p></li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

  </remark>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       QUICK PROSE + UNDERDAMPED SUMMARY ASSEMBLAGE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We now focus on the underdamped case, where the mass oscillates with
    decaying amplitude.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-underdamped-summary">
    <title>Summary: Underdamped Case of <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m></title>
    <tabular halign="left" row-headers="yes">
      <col width="22%"/>
      <col width="36%"/>
      <col width="20%"/>
      <col width="22%"/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Discriminant and roots</cell>
        <cell>Crossings of <m>x=0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} x(t)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><term>Underdamped</term> (smaller <m>c</m>)</cell>
        <cell><m>0 \lt c^2 \lt 4km</m> (complex roots with nonzero real part)</cell>
        <cell>Infinitely many</cell>
        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SETUP: ODE AND SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Consider the underdamped system with <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 4</m>, <m>k = 5</m>,
    <m>x(0) = 2</m>, <m>x'(0) = -2</m>. Substituting these parameters gives the IVP:
    <me>x'' + 4x' + 5x = 0, \quad x(0) = 2,\quad x'(0) = -2.</me>
    The solution to this IVP is:
    <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(2\cos(t) + 2\sin(t)).</me>
  </p>

  <solution>
    <title>Work: Solution to the IVP (can skim)</title>
    <p>
      With <m>m=1</m>, <m>c=4</m>, <m>k=5</m> the characteristic equation is
      <m>r^2 + 4r + 5 = 0</m>. The discriminant is <m>D = 16 - 20 = -4 \lt 0</m>,
      confirming the system is underdamped. By the quadratic formula:
      <me>r = \frac{-4 \pm \sqrt{-4}}{2} = -2 \pm i.</me>
      So <m>\alpha = -2</m> and <m>\omega = 1</m>, giving general solution:
      <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(c_1\cos(t) + c_2\sin(t)).</me>
      Applying <m>x(0) = 2</m>: <m>c_1 = 2</m>.
      Differentiating and applying <m>x'(0) = -2</m>:
      <me>x'(0) = -2c_1 + c_2 = -2 \implies c_2 = -2 + 2(2) = 2.</me>
      So <m>x(t) = e^{-2t}(2\cos(t) + 2\sin(t))</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXPLORATION: DIRECTION CHANGES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exploration xml:id="exploration-direction-changes">
    <title>When Does the Mass Change Direction?</title>

    <task xml:id="task-direction-graph">
      <title>Observe the Graph</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The interactive below shows an underdamped spring alongside the graph
          of its position function. A point moves along the curve as you drag
          the slider, with a tangent line showing the slope <m>x'(t)</m> at
          each moment.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Note:</em> to make the direction changes clearly visible, we use
          a slower decay rate here (<m>\alpha = -0.15</m> instead of <m>-2</m>).
          The qualitative behavior is the same.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-underdamped-direction">
          <caption>
            Drag the slider to move along the curve. Watch the slope of the
            tangent line and the spring position. What happens to the slope
            at a peak or trough?
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-underdamped-direction"
            iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-underdamped-direction.html"
            width="100%" aspect="5:2">
            <description>
              On the left, an animated underdamped spring. On the right, the
              graph of x of t with a moving point and tangent line. A t slider
              controls the position. When the slope is zero the spring canvas
              displays Change in Direction in large red text. Students observe
              that direction changes correspond exactly to where the tangent
              line is horizontal.
            </description>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-direction-mc">
      <title>Connect to Calculus</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Based on your observations above, which equation corresponds exactly
          to the mass changing direction?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>x'(t) = 0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Direction changes occur exactly at critical points of
              <m>x(t)</m>, where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>. Note that for underdamped
              systems, every critical point is a direction change (no saddle
              points), since the solution oscillates. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>x(t) = 0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>x(t) = 0</m> means the mass is at the equilibrium position,
              not that it is changing direction. The mass can pass through
              equilibrium while still moving.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>x''(t) = 0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>x''(t) = 0</m> corresponds to inflection points of <m>x(t)</m>,
              not direction changes.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>x'(t) = x(t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This equation has no direct physical meaning in this context.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-direction-solve">
      <title>Find All Direction Changes</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now find all times <m>t \geq 0</m> at which the mass changes direction.
          Recall <m>x(t) = e^{-2t}(2\cos(t) + 2\sin(t))</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          <alert>Step 1.</alert> Compute <m>x'(t)</m> using the product rule.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Step 2.</alert> Solve <m>x'(t) = 0</m>. Since <m>e^{-2t} \neq 0</m>,
          you can factor it out and solve what remains.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>x'(t) = 0</m> at every direction change, we compute and solve.
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Step 1: Compute <m>x'(t)</m></title>
          <p>
            Using the product rule:
            <md>
              <mrow>x'(t) \amp= -2e^{-2t}(2\cos(t) + 2\sin(t)) + e^{-2t}(-2\sin(t) + 2\cos(t))</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= e^{-2t}\bigl(-4\cos(t) - 4\sin(t) - 2\sin(t) + 2\cos(t)\bigr)</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= e^{-2t}(-2\cos(t) - 6\sin(t)).</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          Now we set <m>x'(t) = 0</m> and solve.
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Step 2: Solve <m>x'(t) = 0</m></title>
          <p>
            Since <m>e^{-2t} \neq 0</m>, we need:
            <me>-2\cos(t) - 6\sin(t) = 0 \implies \tan(t) = -\frac{1}{3}.</me>
            Using the prerequisite remark above with <m>A = -1/3</m>:
            <me>t = \arctan\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{3}\right) + n\pi.</me>
            Since <m>A = -1/3 \lt 0</m> (Case 2), valid
            solutions require <m>n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots</m>, giving:
            <me>t = \arctan\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{3}\right) + n\pi, \quad n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots</me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exploration>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: DIRECTION CHANGES = CRITICAL POINTS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-direction-changes">
    <title>Direction Changes in the Mass-Spring System</title>
    <p>
      In the mass-spring system, direction changes always correspond to critical
      points of <m>x(t)</m>, i.e., times when <m>x'(t) = 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      While in general a function can have critical points that are saddle points
      (not direction changes), this does not happen in the mass-spring system.
      When the velocity <m>x'(t)</m> slows down to zero, the physics of the spring
      forces the mass to turn in the opposite direction: the spring cannot stretch,
      slow to zero velocity, and then continue stretching. So every critical point
      is a genuine direction change.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Direction changes correspond to critical points:</alert>
          the mass changes direction exactly when <m>x'(t) = 0</m>.
          For underdamped systems every critical point is a direction change.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Solving <m>x'(t) = 0</m>:</alert> after computing <m>x'(t)</m>
          and dividing out <m>e^{\alpha t}</m>, we arrive at
          <m>\tan(t) = A</m> for some constant <m>A</m>, giving
          <m>t = \arctan(A) + n\pi</m>. Use the sign of <m>A</m>
          to determine which values of <m>n</m> give <m>t \geq 0</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

